Implement user story AMSD-2041: [GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Story Description
[GO, UP, MX] Live Preview of Content in CMS

## Acceptance Criteria
- 


## Root Cause Analysis & Prescribed Fix (AUTHORITATIVE — start here, do not re-trace)
A code investigation already traced this bug to its cause and prescribed the minimal fix below. This is the plan of record. Apply it; do NOT re-read the whole codebase to re-derive it.

The Acceptance Criteria above describe the desired END BEHAVIOR to VERIFY — they are NOT an implementation blueprint. Do not re-architect, split values, or add new fields/abstractions to satisfy an AC literally when the prescribed minimal fix already makes that AC pass. Implement the fix below; the ACs are how you check you got it right.

HARD RULES:
- Make the SMALLEST change that fixes the root cause. Fewer lines of code is always better.
- REUSE existing functions. Before writing any new helper, search the repo for an existing util/parser/formatter that already does what you need (use the CodeGraph tool documented below) and call it. Writing novel code when a helper already exists is a defect to be rejected in review.
- **src/services/contentstack.ts** (`options`): This is where the Contentstack SDK Stack is initialized. The `options` object passed to `contentstack.Stack()` lacks the `live_preview` configuration property required by the Contentstack Live Preview SDK. Without it, the Stack cannot receive real-time content edits from the CMS, so no downstream component can ever show live preview data — the entire pipeline is dead at the source.
  - **Minimal fix:** Add `live_preview` to the `options` object (lines 55–67) before the Stack is constructed. The Contentstack SDK requires a `live_preview` key with `{ management_token: string, enable: true, host: string }` (values from new env vars e.g. CONTENTSTACK_MANAGEMENT_TOKEN, CONTENTSTACK_PREVIEW_HOST). Then, after Stack creation, call `ContentstackLivePreview.init({ stack: Stack })` (from `@contentstack/live-preview-utils`) to register the listener. The `ContentstackProvider` in `src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx` must also wire `ContentstackLivePreview.addListener()` to update `defaultContent` state on callback, so the context re-renders components with fresh preview data.
- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): The provider currently treats `defaultContent` as a static prop and memoizes it with useMemo. Live Preview requires the provider to reactively update content when the CMS editor changes it — the ContentstackLivePreview listener callback must setState here to push new content into the React tree, causing all 236+ useContent consumers to re-render with updated data.
  - **Minimal fix:** Replace the static `useMemo` with `useState` initialized from `defaultContent`, and register a `ContentstackLivePreview.addListener()` callback (from `@contentstack/live-preview-utils`) inside a `useEffect` that calls the state setter with the updated entry data on each preview change. This makes the entire content pipeline reactive to CMS live edits.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When a tester edits a piece of content in the Contentstack CMS Live Preview panel, the corresponding text visible on the app's page updates to show the edited value without the tester performing a manual or full page reload.
- With the app opened from the Contentstack Live Preview editor, the tester observes that a content change saved in the CMS appears on the running page, whereas before the fix the same edit never appeared until the page was manually refreshed.
- After editing CMS content that is displayed in multiple places on the page (e.g. headings, body text, buttons), the tester observes each of those UI elements showing the newly edited content.
- When a tester changes the title of a card entry in the CMS Live Preview editor, the card rendered on the page displays the new title in place of the old one.
- When a tester changes the destination URL of a link entry in the CMS Live Preview editor and then clicks that link on the page, the browser navigates to the newly set destination.
- After making several successive edits to the same content in the CMS, the tester observes the page showing the latest saved value each time, not an earlier stale version.
- While live preview edits are being applied on the page, the tester observes no new error messages in the browser console and the rest of the page remains functional.
- When the app is viewed outside the Contentstack Live Preview editor (normal browsing), the tester observes the published content rendering correctly with no change in behaviour from before this work.

